WEBVTT

00:00.270 --> 00:01.740
Instructor: Okay, so in this video,

00:01.740 --> 00:04.050
I wanna quickly talk about a common issue

00:04.050 --> 00:06.510
that some students run into early on,

00:06.510 --> 00:09.480
and to explain it, first we need to understand

00:09.480 --> 00:12.810
that when we have a Git repo, we initialize a Git repo,

00:12.810 --> 00:15.000
that's in one directory, right?

00:15.000 --> 00:18.420
In our case, it was the MyFirstNovel directory.

00:18.420 --> 00:21.660
But Git is going to watch everything that happens

00:21.660 --> 00:26.070
in this directory, including anything nested further down,

00:26.070 --> 00:29.700
as you know, child or grandchild directories and so on.

00:29.700 --> 00:32.910
So I could just quickly make some dummy folders.

00:32.910 --> 00:35.640
I'm gonna delete these, so you do not need to follow along.

00:35.640 --> 00:37.683
If you are, just gonna call this,

00:38.580 --> 00:40.080
let's say I'm working on the book.

00:40.080 --> 00:43.830
Here's the intro to the book, I'll cd into the intro,

00:43.830 --> 00:46.470
and then I'll make another folder in here.

00:46.470 --> 00:48.603
How about Draft1?

00:49.710 --> 00:53.940
Okay, so I'm two folders in, two levels deep,

00:53.940 --> 00:55.800
but I can still type git status-

00:55.800 --> 00:58.170
Whoops, apparently not.

00:58.170 --> 00:59.820
If I type it correctly,

00:59.820 --> 01:01.680
and I still get feedback that says,

01:01.680 --> 01:03.810
yes, you are in a Git repo.

01:03.810 --> 01:07.320
So it's not just this folder, MyFirstNovel,

01:07.320 --> 01:10.590
it's everything nested in that folder.

01:10.590 --> 01:12.180
So it's top down.

01:12.180 --> 01:15.720
And that's important to understand for a couple of reasons.

01:15.720 --> 01:18.630
One, any files and folders and changes I do

01:18.630 --> 01:20.430
in this directory for this one project

01:20.430 --> 01:24.240
are tracked by Git if I initialize the repo like I did.

01:24.240 --> 01:26.400
But two, it's important to understand,

01:26.400 --> 01:30.120
we do not want to be initializing another repo

01:30.120 --> 01:32.490
inside of an existing repo.

01:32.490 --> 01:35.280
I made a warning sign to make this clear.

01:35.280 --> 01:37.680
We wanna keep our repositories separate,

01:37.680 --> 01:40.140
and you don't want to run git init

01:40.140 --> 01:42.330
inside of an existing repository.

01:42.330 --> 01:44.220
It's not the end of the world,

01:44.220 --> 01:46.320
but at some point, things will go wrong.

01:46.320 --> 01:47.580
Git will get confused,

01:47.580 --> 01:50.550
because it's tracking everything happening in a folder,

01:50.550 --> 01:53.880
and if one of those folders has Git also initialized,

01:53.880 --> 01:58.140
then it's Git tracking Git, and it just gets confused,

01:58.140 --> 02:00.030
so we wanna avoid that.

02:00.030 --> 02:03.030
And the easiest thing to do is just make sure,

02:03.030 --> 02:06.630
when you run git init, make sure you're not in a repository.

02:06.630 --> 02:10.080
And that's why I showed you to use git status first.

02:10.080 --> 02:12.270
So if I wanted to make a new repository,

02:12.270 --> 02:15.840
I'm writing a new novel, I want to have a separate history,

02:15.840 --> 02:17.640
I would try running git status.

02:17.640 --> 02:19.710
Oh, I'm in a repo.

02:19.710 --> 02:23.460
That should tell me, don't initialize a new repo in here.

02:23.460 --> 02:25.050
You're already in a repo.

02:25.050 --> 02:26.910
Go somewhere else.

02:26.910 --> 02:28.560
So let's back out a couple levels.

02:28.560 --> 02:29.850
Let's keep going.

02:29.850 --> 02:32.880
So I've backed out, and now I can make another directory,

02:32.880 --> 02:36.960
you know, Book2, go into that directory.

02:36.960 --> 02:38.160
Just show it over here.

02:38.160 --> 02:39.240
Here it is.

02:39.240 --> 02:41.520
And type git status.

02:41.520 --> 02:43.290
Not a Git repository.

02:43.290 --> 02:45.060
Now I can initialize.

02:45.060 --> 02:46.710
All right, so you just wanna be careful of that.

02:46.710 --> 02:48.570
If you do happen to initialize a repo

02:48.570 --> 02:50.490
inside of an existing repo,

02:50.490 --> 02:54.180
I showed you how to remove that repository

02:54.180 --> 02:58.650
by getting rid of the corresponding .git folder.

02:58.650 --> 02:59.940
All right, so it's not the end of the world,

02:59.940 --> 03:01.140
it won't give you an error message,

03:01.140 --> 03:02.940
but you wanna be careful,

03:02.940 --> 03:07.320
And in general, my rule is, make a folder for a project

03:07.320 --> 03:09.600
and then initialize Git in that folder.

03:09.600 --> 03:12.261
So you don't wanna have a Git repo on your desktop

03:12.261 --> 03:15.270
or your documents folder or on your entire machine

03:15.270 --> 03:16.920
that's tracking everything.

03:16.920 --> 03:19.290
You could, but most people prefer

03:19.290 --> 03:21.690
to have one repo per project.

03:21.690 --> 03:23.190
So always run git status.

03:23.190 --> 03:25.800
Make sure you're not in a repo before you initialize,

03:25.800 --> 03:28.170
and if you do happen to end up with nested repos,

03:28.170 --> 03:31.590
you can sort it out by deleting one of the .git folders.

03:31.590 --> 03:32.423
Okay?
